y\n\nWe might be interested in the inverse problem. That is, given a stable real polynomial f,  can we find two other stable polynomials whose Hadamard product is f?\n\n### Definition 5\n\nLet \\(f\\in \\mathbb {R}[x]\\) be stable. If f can be represented as \\(f=p*q,\\) for certain stable polynomials \\(p,q\\in \\mathbb {R}[x],\\) we say that f has a stable Hadamard factorization.\n\n### Example 6\n\nConsider the following system with control u(t):\n\n$$\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{l} \\dot{x}_1(t)=-(k+2)x_1(t)-(2k+3)x_2(t)\\\\ \\dot{x}_2(t)=(k+1)x_1(t)-u(t) \\end{array} \\right. , $$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The Hadamard Product of Two Stable Multivariate Polynomials is not Necessarily Stable (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/250607375_The_Hadamard_Product_of_Two_Stable_Multivariate_Polynomials_is_not_Necessarily_Stable)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; In this note we demonstrate by a counterexample that the Hadamard product of two stable multivariate polynomials is not necessarily a stable multivariate polynomial. ... T HEOREM 4: [1] The Hadamard product of two Hurwitz stable polynomials is Hurwitz\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA001181 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A001181/internal)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Zhao, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.05873\">Analytic properties arising from the Baxter numbers</a>, arXiv:2505.05873 [math.CO], 2025.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComments on ‘On Hadamard powers of polynomials’ | Mathematics of Control, Signals, and Systems | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00498-017-0202-0)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; We start this subsection with a simple characterization of stable polynomials of degree 4 with positive co